::heading level=1 id=dx-project-index
dx — project index
::end

::paragraph id=paragraph-2
dx is a document format, a store, and a toolchain: a `.dx` document is block-structured, renders to a page, and can execute the code blocks inside it. `README.md` is the front door, `CLAUDE.md` is the working contract, and `docs/dx-format-contract.dx` is the authority on format behavior. This index is the map: each area below says what it is for, the load-bearing files render as their current text (`::code src=`), and the verify block at the bottom holds the map to the tree mechanically — it re-runs whenever the workspace changes, so this page cannot quietly go stale.
::end

::heading level=2 id=now
Now
::end

::paragraph id=now-note
The working section — first read of a turn, last write. The worklist is the task's program counter; thoughts land here the moment they form (dx_append, dx_check) and promote into the sections below when they harden. A closed line is deleted, not kept: its record is the part's commit message (`git log`) — a worklist that carries its own history charges every orientation read for it.
::end

::checklist id=now-worklist
[ ] Store distribution: sign and submit the browser extension archives — submit `packaging/build/dx-firefox.xpi` to addons.mozilla.org (unlisted) → `packaging/signed/`, upload `dx-chrome.zip` to the Chrome Web Store and set `CHROME_WEB_STORE` in `extension.rs`, Apple Developer for Safari
[x] Field test: rebuild a session gallery on ::image for= with the supersampled reads
[x] Verify a private repository renders on github.com in a signed-in browser
[x] The tier past one-launch: a capture session held across calls and delta-frame reads. Design constraint, learned part 50: the session must be owned by a host that drops it — MCP's handle() is a pure request→response function, and a process-wide static would orphan a headless Chromium on every hard exit — so this lands as a deliberate server-state change, never a static
[ ] `dx search` still reads and tokenises the whole tree on every call — now in parallel, and at 33 ms warm here rather than 114 (`validation.dx#tool-cost`), which is where the cheap wins ran out. The profile that ended them: `docs 27 ms / source 9 ms / index 59 ms`, so the stage this line used to blame was never the expensive one. Skipping the work needs a persistent source index, and the honest blocker is the contract: a read may not write one, so it belongs to `dx sync`/`dx index`, with the scan as the fallback when the cache is stale
[ ] First contact lands 4/8 against grep's 7/8 (`validation.dx#first-contact`), and the four misses are no longer one kind of failure. Two want a fact the corpus states in a table or a constant rather than in a sentence — *what sampling rate does recorded audio arrive at* wants `16000` from a `#define`, and a scorer over prose has nothing to rank a bare number by. One (*how do I compile the native engine*) `rg` misses too, so it is not a search failure. The last, *what checks the one bit network still works*, asks for a file by what it verifies: the glossary carries *checks* to `test`, and the test file still loses, because its name is the only place the subject appears — a filename is title-weighted already, so the fix is not another weight
[ ] The glossary (`doc-core::search::VOCABULARY`) is hand-written general computing English and will always be short of somebody's word. The corpus can state its own equivalences — prose that writes "graphics processing unit (GPU)" is a definition — and reading those out of the documents would extend the bridge into each project's vocabulary without anyone maintaining a table
[ ] Only the *first* token on a fresh machine still crosses by hand (`dx report token <t>`, minted on the box with `selfhost reports token`): within a machine, `dx report setup` now needs nothing — it stores one token machine-wide, adopts one an existing subscription already carries, and mints a collision-resistant project key of its own. What would remove the last step: setup reading the token over the SSH the operator already has, the way the Self-Host console fetches the box's admin token
[x] Close the first-contact gap from the scaffold side too, not only the ranker: a deterministic, token-free project-outline tool (a symbol index, a reference graph — Helpers' `index_project`/`project_map` is one example) can fill `dx_index`'s TODOs with real facts instead of file-tree heuristics alone, cheap enough to run on every setup. The MCP handshake (`rust/doc-cli/src/mcp/mod.rs`, ORIENT) now tells agents to reach for one when it's on hand and to fall back to a literal source search — writing what it finds straight into the document — when `dx_search` still misses, so the cold-repo score climbs from a plain search's floor toward landing every question instead of sitting at 4/8
[ ] `docs/index-write-back.dx` has the contract for editing a rendered index and having it write source (three tiers: rename propagation, annotation correction, index-as-source-of-truth) — the stated exception CLAUDE.md's "reading never writes" would need, drafted and ready. Tier A's hard prerequisite is a real reference graph; that's what `dx trace` (below) is for. Not implemented — B and C each need their own grilling pass first (see `#iwb-open`)
[x] Non-operator machines cannot yet claim scoped report tokens; `dx report setup` without local `selfhost` access falls back to token adoption (from existing subscriptions or manual provision). A future account-based self-serve mechanism — such as `dx login` authenticated to the box — could mint per-project tokens for non-operators, but requires endpoint changes and is deferred past this pass to focus on the operator case where the bot runs the box.
[ ] Reduce open reports from 19 to zero: prioritize report-7f47c2b4 (sandbox macOS temp dir), report-884d9b9c (MCP/CLI clobber), report-8080ad4d (gitignore), report-42ca9802 (capture localhost), report-5a5091cf (dxcp unreadable)
[x] Verify dx sync handles merge conflicts correctly when two agents edit .doc/repo.dxcp concurrently; run conflict scenario tests and document behavior
[ ] Complete editor surface polish: ensure all block types render correctly, keyboard navigation works smoothly, and undo/redo is reliable in both light and dark themes
[x] Document dx CLI comprehensively with examples for each command (read, write, search, render, run); update help text where unclear
[ ] Audit SARA integration: confirm index.dx is read correctly each engine tick, verify checklist parsing is robust, test escalateOnRetry behavior
[ ] Add missing coverage for edge cases in .dx format parsing: empty blocks, deeply nested headings, Unicode content; create tests in Rust and verify pass
::end

::heading level=2 id=the-map
The map
::end

::paragraph id=paragraph-4
The engine is one Rust core; everything a person or agent touches is a surface fed by it.
::end

::paragraph id=mermaid-5-core hidden
doc-core - the format and its views
::end

