::heading level=2 id=rust-the-engine
rust/ — the engine
::end

::paragraph id=paragraph-7
The only thing on a user's or agent's path. Six crates, one responsibility each: `doc-core` is the format and its views (parse, render, edit, resolve, pointer, chunk — no OS dependencies, compiles to wasm), `doc-store` is the SQLite chunk store and the resolver that always hands back the true document, `doc-run` executes `::code … run` blocks inside a kernel sandbox (`confine`), `doc-shot` renders pages and blocks to PNG through Chromium, `doc-cli` is the `dx` binary (CLI, MCP server, `dx serve`, installer), and `doc-wasm` is `doc-core` for JavaScript hosts. The workspace, live:
::end

::code id=workspace lang=toml src=rust/Cargo.toml

::end

::heading level=2 id=build-heading
Build
::end

::paragraph id=build-para
To build the Rust engine, run `cargo build --release` from the `rust/` directory:

```
cd rust
export PATH="$HOME/.cargo/bin:$PATH"
cargo build --release
```

All six crates compile together. The `doc-cli` binary lands in `target/release/dx`.
::end

::heading level=2 id=test-heading
Test
::end

::paragraph id=test-para
Run the test suite with `cargo test` from the `rust/` directory:

```
cd rust
export PATH="$HOME/.cargo/bin:$PATH"
cargo test
```

All tests must pass. Use `cargo test --package <crate>` to test a single crate. Check formatting and lints with:

```
cargo fmt --check
cargo clippy --all-targets -- -D warnings
```
::end

::heading level=2 id=entry-points-heading
Entry Points
::end

::paragraph id=entry-points-para
Start with these entry points depending on what you want to work on:

- **Format and rendering** — `doc-core/src/format.rs`: parse, stringify, and render the .dx format
- **Store and resolver** — `doc-store/src/lib.rs`: chunk storage and the resolver that always hands back the true document  
- **Execution sandbox** — `doc-run/src/lib.rs`: confined block execution on macOS and Linux
- **Page capture** — `doc-shot/src/lib.rs`: Chromium-based page and block rendering to PNG
- **CLI and MCP** — `doc-cli/src/main.rs`: the `dx` binary, MCP server, and `dx serve` daemon
- **WASM engine** — `doc-wasm/src/lib.rs`: compiles `doc-core` to JavaScript WebAssembly

Each crate's module documentation explains its responsibilities. Read `CLAUDE.md` for the working contract and `docs/method.dx` for the full development method.
::end

::heading level=2 id=editor-the-surfaces
editor/ — the surfaces
::end

::paragraph id=paragraph-10
`editor/surface` is the editor, and there is one of it — `edit.js`/`edit.css` are what happens when a reader clicks a block, copied verbatim into each host by the build scripts so no host owns a line of it. What each host *does* own is the bridge, and the calls it must answer are exactly the ones `edit.js` makes: `editor/vscode/test/host-contract.test.mjs` holds the surface's doc, the VS Code bridge, and DX.app's to one list. `editor/vscode` is the VS Code extension (renders through `doc-wasm`, byte-identical to the CLI). `editor/github` is the browser extension that makes github.com show documents instead of pointers; its logic lives in `resolve.js`, tested under node against a pack a real `dx` wrote. One command builds both wasm engines — a surface built at a different moment is a surface running a different renderer:
::end

::code id=build-surfaces lang=sh src=editor/build.sh

::end

::heading level=2 id=packaging-what-a-person-installs
packaging/ — what a person installs
::end

::paragraph id=paragraph-13
Release tools, never part of the shipped binary: `build-app.sh` assembles `DX.app` (the Mac viewer — AppKit, a `WKWebView`, and its own bundled `dx`), `build-stores.sh` the Chrome Web Store archive and the XPI Mozilla signs, `build-safari.sh` the Safari extension. `packaging/packaging.dx` is the release runbook and the record of what each browser actually permits.
::end

::heading level=2 id=docs-the-contracts
docs/ — the contracts
::end

::paragraph id=paragraph-15
`dx-format-contract.dx` is the authority on format behavior — canonical output, block grammar, the reference rules, and the close-token escape that lets a document carry dx syntax. `github.dx` explains why the GitHub extension resolves in the reader's browser. `board-geometry.dx` is the account of `render::board`'s public door — `edge_layout`/`drag_path` — and how each host reaches it: the CSP each needs, what it grants, and the degradation before it loads. `grill-me.dx` is the review checklist for parser, renderer, and save-path changes. `intake.dx` is the report box's wire contract — the three routes, the id both ends compute, and what the box owes a reporter — so the box can be built or moved without reading the client. `method.dx` is how the project is worked: orientation, read routes, and the harness that proves a claim. Every one of them is a document now, so search reaches them and a verdict can be recorded in them.
::end

::heading level=2 id=engine-contracts
The engine's contracts
::end

::paragraph id=contracts-note
The non-negotiables, in the engine's own words — each names the module that owns it, and the check below holds every stated word against the code, so this section cannot quietly drift.
::end

::heading level=3 id=contract-pointer
A .dx file on disk: the pointer and its digest
::end

::paragraph id=contract-pointer-text
A `.dx` file on disk is one line — `~ dx1 <64-hex digest>` — the pointer `doc-core::pointer` teaches every surface to recognize alike. The digest is the sha256 hash of the document's canonical text, computed by `doc-core::digest`, so the file changes exactly when the content changes and git still tracks every edit. The content lives in `.doc/repo.dxcp` as content-addressed chunks; every chunk digest uses the same sha256, a chunk holds the exact canonical text of one block, and reassembly is concatenation — storage cannot lose a byte. The committed pack is written *uncompressed* on purpose (`chunk::PackStorage`): git deltas plain bytes between revisions and cannot delta a compressed stream, so the policy that wins on disk loses in history, measured twenty-to-one.
::end

::heading level=3 id=contract-confinement
Confinement: the sandbox around a running block
::end

::paragraph id=contract-confinement-text
Running a document does not give it your authority. The crate that confines code execution is `doc-run` — its `confine` module puts every block inside a kernel sandbox, Seatbelt on macOS and bubblewrap on Linux. The sandbox reads the repository the document lives in, the run caches, and the system toolchains — never the rest of your files, and the known credential stores are denied by name, a deny-list the module itself calls best-effort; it writes the block's own directory plus the `writes=` folders the block declares; and the network stays gone while a block's own code runs — only dependency install may fetch, arranged in `setup` so no `run` command ever downloads. A machine that cannot impose the boundary does not run the block; `DX_UNCONFINED=1` is the only way past and says so in its output. `lang=capture` is the one deliberate exception: there is no interpreter and no subprocess of the block's own code to confine, so `doc-run/src/live.rs` scopes it a different way — a browser that resolves and proxies only the one `target=` host it was told to open, hostname or IP literal alike.
::end

::heading level=3 id=contract-approvals
Approvals: the fingerprint ledger
::end

::paragraph id=contract-approvals-text
Unreviewed code does not run at all — but a local edit is the review. `doc-run::approvals` is a ledger of fingerprints this machine recorded, never anything inside a repository. What goes into an approval fingerprint is the code and its powers: the runner, the declared deps, the exact code text, the `reads=` paths as declared, and the `writes=` grant — deliberately not the read files' current text. Every local editing surface records the approval as it saves; a document's own `::output` record can approve nothing, and `--force` is the one way past, stamped into the output it produces so a bypass is never silent.
::end

::heading level=3 id=contract-escape
Escaping: what author markup survives
::end

::paragraph id=contract-escape-text
Untrusted input is everything a document carries. Author markup survives `render::escape` only by being on its allow-list — an allow-list of elements, attributes, and URL schemes, never a deny-list, because the module doc records the payloads that defeated the deny-list it replaced. A shadow root is not a boundary; the one real boundary the renderer uses is a `::view`'s engine-built `<iframe sandbox="">`, and that attribute stays empty forever.
::end

::heading level=3 id=contract-board
Board geometry: stated, never measured
::end

::paragraph id=contract-board-text
Board geometry lives in `render::board`, and the rule is: a node's box is stated, never measured. `x y w h` are the whole of it, a dimension may state a rule (`page`/`fit`) and keeps the word forever, and the geometry — sides, spread, curve, clearance, overlap — is stated once there and asked for again, never re-derived, through `edge_layout`/`drag_path`: the door `doc-wasm` opens for an editing surface to route a board against boxes it measured instead of the ones a static render had to guess (`docs/board-geometry.dx`). A `::mermaid` flowchart is read at parse time and arranged into this same board, so a second diagram renderer never exists to disagree.
::end

::heading level=3 id=contract-search
Search: how a question meets a word
::end

::paragraph id=contract-search-text
A question is asked in the reader's words and answered by the project's, so `doc-core::search` indexes each word under the tokens it could be asked for: its stem (`weights` and `weight` meet, `string` keeps its `ing` because the remainder is not a word), and the parts of a compound name (`packWeights` is reachable as `pack` and as `weights`, at half weight — a part is weaker evidence than the word). A query token no unit holds at all, and only then, also scores against its kin at a discount: the words that carry it inside them and the abbreviation it starts with, which is how `packed` reaches `bitpack.c` and `microphone` reaches `mic.py`. Where the words share no letters at all, a stated glossary of general computing English (`VOCABULARY`) carries the question across — *graphics card* to `gpu`, *folder* to `directory` — as terms of their own at a discount, so bridging a word never makes the word look common and stop selecting. Ranking is one BM25 scorer over field-weighted frequencies for documents and blocks alike; captured `::output` weighs half, because a record of a run quotes whatever was asked of it and must not outrank the prose that answers. A term more than half the corpus holds may add to a score but may not carry one, so a question this corpus cannot answer says so instead of returning whatever said "the". The block a hit hands back is the one that *states* the fact: a heading is a label, and so is a test, which repeats a query more often than the line declaring the thing — and the excerpt is cut at the line that answers, because a hit on the right block showing the wrong sentence reads as a miss.
::end

::heading level=3 id=contract-coverage
Search coverage: a discarded fact, kept
::end

::paragraph id=contract-coverage-text
`workspace::search()` already knows, for every query, whether its top hit landed on a document or fell back to source — and used to discard that fact the moment it returned. `doc-cli::coverage` appends one best-effort record to `.doc/coverage.jsonl` instead: the query, and where the hit landed. The log is self-pruning, is never created by a workspace `dx` has not already touched, and its writes never fail a search or slow one down measurably. `dx coverage`/`dx_coverage` report the document-hit rate over a window plus the fallback queries, most-repeated first, and `--min-rate` makes the rate a normal dx gate — reporting "no data" rather than failing when a project has not been searched yet (`docs/search-coverage.dx`).
::end

::code id=contracts-verify lang=sh run reads=rust/doc-core/src/pointer.rs,rust/doc-core/src/digest.rs,rust/doc-run/src/confine.rs,rust/doc-run/src/approvals.rs,rust/doc-core/src/render/escape.rs,rust/doc-core/src/render/board.rs,rust/doc-core/src/search.rs,editor/surface/edit.js,rust/doc-cli/src/coverage.rs
fail=0
holds() { # holds <file> <word...> — every stated word still stands in its owning module
  f="$1"; shift
  for word in "$@"; do
    grep -qi -- "$word" "$f" || { echo "FAIL $f no longer says: $word"; fail=1; }
  done
}
holds rust/doc-core/src/pointer.rs dx1
holds rust/doc-core/src/digest.rs SHA-256
holds rust/doc-run/src/confine.rs Seatbelt bubblewrap network best-effort
holds rust/doc-run/src/approvals.rs fingerprint
holds rust/doc-core/src/render/escape.rs allow-list
holds rust/doc-core/src/render/board.rs stated measured
holds rust/doc-core/src/search.rs stem abbreviation kin
holds rust/doc-cli/src/coverage.rs jsonl best-effort
# `docs/search-coverage.dx`'s central claim, checked where a block can actually reach the
# engine and the CLI it drives: coverage.rs is the only writer of .doc/coverage.jsonl.
grep -c "coverage.jsonl" rust/doc-cli/src/coverage.rs >/dev/null || {
  echo "FAIL rust/doc-cli/src/coverage.rs no longer names its own log file"
  fail=1
}
# `docs/board-geometry.dx`'s central claim, checked where a block can actually reach both
# trees: the geometry deleted from the surface has not crept back in.
for name in cubicAt curveBetween controlsFor leadOf autoSides clearestSides crossingSine \
  hiddenSamples midpointOn boxHolds segmentHits reachOf spansAcross anchorOn labelSpot centreOf; do
  grep -q "function $name(" editor/surface/edit.js && {
    echo "FAIL editor/surface/edit.js still defines $name — the geometry belongs to render::board now"
    fail=1
  }
done
grep -q "board_edge_layout" editor/surface/edit.js || { echo "FAIL edit.js never calls board_edge_layout"; fail=1; }
[ "$fail" = 0 ] && echo "ok — each contract's words still stand in its owning module"
exit $fail
::end

::output id=contracts-verify-output for=contracts-verify status=ok hash=8a2d21d458a5096cfc9ebbb11edb5d424c989b57006ecf415c7e610cd9c7532b
ok — each contract's words still stand in its owning module
::end

::heading level=2 id=examples-the-corpus
examples/ — the corpus
::end

::paragraph id=paragraph-17
Read `GETTING_STARTED.dx` first; `tutorial.dx` teaches the format, `showcase.dx` and `block-reference.dx` show every block kind. `example_site_plan.dx` is the flagship: a real site (`site/`) designed on a board and held to its spec by a run block. `route-economics.dx` measures the token economy of reading routes with dx itself. The two `_template.dx` files are starting boards. All are pointers; their content lives in the committed pack at `.doc/repo.dxcp`.
::end

::heading level=2 id=dev-dx-the-harness
dev.dx — the harness
::end

::paragraph id=paragraph-19
One document proves the repository: `dx run dev.dx` runs the engine suites, the lints, both JS surface suites, corpus resolution, and the rendered-page contract inside the sandbox, and records each verdict. What must watch from outside — the attack payloads and the Chromium captures — is stated there as the host-shell command it is. `validation.dx` prices the method itself against this repository: what a verdict costs to read against the log it replaces, what the documents answer against what `CLAUDE.md` answers, and the sandbox proving its own boundary from inside.
::end

::heading level=2 id=reports-section
reports.dx — what using dx turned up
::end

::paragraph id=reports-section-text
The field reports: bugs, suggestions, and observations about dx, filed by agents *using* it
from whatever project they were in. `dx_report` writes one into this machine's inbox —
outside every repository, because the checkout is almost never the project the reporter is
standing in — and then pushes it to the **intake**, a public endpoint on the box that holds
the database every subscribed checkout reads. This checkout is subscribed, so
[`reports.dx`](reports.dx) carries what everyone filed: `dx report sync` folds the open ones
in, and an MCP session does the same on its own timer, so an agent here inherits them without
running anything. **Nothing waits on a person now** — the old "drain at the start of a
session" is the offline path (`dx report drain`), for a machine that could not reach the
intake.

One block per defect, keyed `report-<fingerprint>` over kind, title, and route, so the same
thing reported twice is a second `seen` line and the sighting count is the priority. Fix it
with `dx report close <id>` — which removes the block *and* closes the record upstream, so no
later sync folds it back — and let the commit message be the record, the same discipline the
worklist follows. `rust/doc-cli/src/intake.rs` is the authority on the route and
`rust/doc-cli/src/reports.rs` on the inbox; the intake itself is `crates/reports` in the
Self-Host repository, stated in its `surfaces.dx` §16 and `docs/SECURITY.md` §3.8. Findings,
below, is the other ledger and stays distinct: those are this repository's own open defects,
held mechanically.
::end

::heading level=2 id=findings
Findings
::end

::paragraph id=paragraph-21
A ledger of open defects and sharp edges. A claim that can be checked mechanically is written as a `::code run` block that checks it, declaring the files it judges with `reads=` — the recorded verdict goes stale the moment those files change, and every read re-runs it. Prose bullets only for what no check can hold. Delete findings when fixed.
::end

::paragraph id=bulleted-list-52
No open findings right now — every dx behavior worth flagging from this session went through `dx_report` instead of a hand-kept note here, and lands in `reports.dx` (currently 0 open — `report-b1031ce7` and `report-ed299c79` closed 2026-08-14: the geometry duplication is fixed by `docs/board-geometry.dx`'s door, and the sync/close reconciliation gap by `intake::fold`'s converse pass).
::end

::paragraph id=paragraph-22
The map above, held mechanically — every workspace member is a real crate, every surface file exists, every document is a pointer, and the fixture corpus stays plain text:
::end

::code id=verify lang=sh run reads=rust/Cargo.toml
fail=0
crates=0
for c in $(sed -n 's/^members = \[\(.*\)\]/\1/p' rust/Cargo.toml | tr -d '",[]'); do
  crates=$((crates + 1))
  [ -d "rust/$c/src" ] || { echo "FAIL crate named in Cargo.toml has no src/: rust/$c"; fail=1; }
done
[ "$crates" -eq 6 ] || { echo "FAIL expected 6 workspace members, found $crates"; fail=1; }
for f in editor/surface/edit.js editor/surface/edit.css editor/build.sh \
         editor/github/resolve.js editor/vscode/package.json \
         packaging/build-app.sh packaging/packaging.dx; do
  [ -e "$f" ] || { echo "FAIL surface file missing: $f"; fail=1; }
done
# The documentation is documents: no Markdown may creep back in beside them.
for f in docs/*.md packaging/README.md rust/README.md rust/ANALYSIS.md; do
  [ -e "$f" ] && { echo "FAIL documentation returned to Markdown: $f"; fail=1; }
done
for f in examples/*.dx docs/*.dx packaging/*.dx rust/*.dx index.dx dev.dx validation.dx reports.dx; do
  # reports.dx exists only while a report is open — every other name here always does.
  [ -e "$f" ] || continue
  head -n1 "$f" | grep -q '^~ dx1 ' || { echo "FAIL document is not a pointer: $f"; fail=1; }
done
for f in rust/doc-core/tests/fixtures/*.dx; do
  head -n1 "$f" | grep -q '^~ dx1 ' && { echo "FAIL fixture became a pointer: $f"; fail=1; }
done
[ "$fail" -eq 0 ] && echo "ok - map holds: $crates crates, 7 surface files, every document a pointer, fixtures plain text"
exit $fail
::end

::output id=verify-output for=verify status=ok hash=9ecd2b3a36c583221fc390af412dd5cf81863e900f24039e7e517f7f72686c63
ok - map holds: 6 crates, 7 surface files, every document a pointer, fixtures plain text
::end

::heading level=1 id=dx-document-format-and-autonomous-tool
dx — document format and autonomous tool
::end

::paragraph id=paragraph-59
The .dx document format, store, CLI, app and editor surfaces enable autonomous agents and humans to work through living documents. A .dx file is a structured format with Markdown prose and embedded runnable blocks; the store manages version history and concurrent edits; the CLI reads, renders, and manipulates documents programmatically; and SARA's autonomous engine consults the index.dx to coordinate work across the codebase. This project is complete and live when the format is robust, all CLI operations are reliable, the editor surface is polished, and SARA can operate autonomously through dx documents with no manual intervention required.
::end

